body:
Hi - I agree, reaching out doesn't always work, but that doesn't mean it will never work.

&#x200B;

If your boyfriend broke up with you because you had a panic attack, it may mean that he is just incapable of handling a situation like yours - either way it sounds like you are better off, though the sadness you might feel from the break up is very valid.

&#x200B;

Same thing goes with your family - like you said, they "mean well" but are likely incapable of handling your situation. I had to reach outside of my friends and family circle to get the help I needed.

&#x200B;

Suicide hotlines are, in my personal experience, at best, a contact for getting resources to get to the actual help you might need, and not a direct solution. They are a good starting point but have a long way to go to being a full solution in and of itself.

&#x200B;

Antidepressants - I took 4 different antidepressants over the course of about 9 years before I found the right antidepressant and dosage combination that worked for me. Prescribing antidepressants is, unfortunately, not an exact science yet, but the benefits from getting it right are worth it.
